## 9. Planned migration: dedicated Hetzner project (`psmdb-bazel-rbe`)

> Status: planned. New Hetzner project already created (id 14460169).
> Cutover sequence below is to be executed AFTER PSMDB-2055 v8.3 build is
> green on the current shared project and the matching cherry-picks are
> merged across `master`, `v8.3`, and `v8.0`.

### 9.1 Why split

- Hetzner Cloud API rate-limit is **per-project, default 3600 req/hr**,
  refilling at 1 req/sec
  (https://docs.hetzner.cloud/reference/cloud#description/rate-limiting).
- The current shared project hosts 6 Jenkins instances (psmdb +
  5 others) plus the BuildBarn control plane (scaler, ondemand workers,
  central preflight). Observed quota drain to **2 / 3600 within seconds**
  during multi-stage parallel builds — once exhausted, the next
  `hcloud server create` fails for ~tens of minutes regardless of which
  team triggered the burst. Cross-team blast radius.
- Volume quota is **also per-project** and currently 850 / 1024 GB used
  in the shared project, which makes any in-place rsync-based volume
  migration impossible (no headroom for a parallel 750 GB volume).

Splitting BB into its own project gives us:
- Independent 3600 req/hr budget — net effective ~2× capacity.
- Independent volume quota — full 1024 GB available for CAS growth.
- Operational isolation — bursts from any of the 5 unrelated Jenkins
  instances no longer starve the BB control plane, and vice versa.

### 9.2 Hetzner constraints (sanity-checked before planning)

| Asset | Cross-project? | Migration path |
|---|---|---|
| Project | n/a | New project `psmdb-bazel-rbe` (id 14460169) created. |
| API token | per-project | Generate fresh in the new project. |
| SSH keys | per-project | Re-upload public keys under the same names. |
| Private network | per-project | Create new network in the new project (CIDR may stay `10.0.0.0/16`; nothing connects across). |
| Floating IP | per-project | Allocate a fresh FIP in `hel1`. |
| Volume (xfs CAS, 750 GB) | NOT cross-project | Cold rebuild of CAS — see §9.3 below. |
| Server | per-project | Spin up fresh `bb-psmdb-ondemand` in new project. |
| TLS cert | DNS-bound | Re-issue Let's Encrypt cert on the new central before DNS cutover. |
| DNS hostname (`bb-psmdb.ddns.net`) | external (No-IP) | Single A-record swap to new FIP — Bazel/CI configs untouched. |

### 9.3 Volume strategy: cold rebuild

CAS volume cannot be moved (volumes are project-local; Hetzner has no
"snapshot" feature for detachable volumes — only for server boot disks
— and Volume Backup is also project-local).

Path of least resistance:
1. Create a fresh empty 750 GB xfs volume in the new project under name
   `psmdb-buildbarn-cas-ondemand`.
2. Run `create-central.sh` with the new project's auth — comes up empty.
3. First Bazel build pre-warms CAS (~3 hours, one-time cost — measured
   against the cold-cache 25-min runs we observed before the warm
   cache was established).
4. Subsequent builds resume normal speed (cache hit rates climb back
   into the 60-90 % band after a single full release matrix).

Old volume is detached but **kept** in the shared project for 1–2 weeks
as rollback insurance, then deleted (frees 750 GB of the 850/1024 GB
shared quota for other teams).

### 9.4 Cutover sequence (operator runbook)

1. Wait for PSMDB-2055 build to finish on shared project; confirm
   green baseline.
2. Merge PSMDB-2034 / PSMDB-2054 changes across `master`, `v8.3`, `v8.0`
   (cherry-picks already prepared on test branches).
3. **In the new project (no impact on shared / production)**:
   - Create Hetzner API token; store under
     `~/.config/psmdb-rbe/hcloud.token` (mode `0600`).
   - Upload SSH public keys: `bb-psmdb-laptop` (operator's pubkey).
     `bb-psmdb-ondemand-central` is generated and uploaded
     automatically by `create-central.sh::ensure_central_ssh_key()`.
   - Create private network `psmdb-bazel-rbe` (CIDR `10.0.0.0/16`).
   - Allocate Floating IP in `hel1` with label `bb-psmdb-central`.
   - Create empty 750 GB xfs volume `psmdb-buildbarn-cas-ondemand`
     in `hel1` (`hcloud volume create --location hel1 --size 750
     --format xfs --name psmdb-buildbarn-cas-ondemand`).
4. Apply `create-central.sh` lookup-by-name patch (see §10).
5. Create operator-local `~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env` (see §10).
6. Re-issue TLS cert for the new central host. Two options:
   a. **Stage cert before DNS cutover** — temporarily point
      `bb-psmdb.ddns.net` at the NEW FIP for ~5 minutes during the
      LE HTTP-01 challenge, then revert until the rest of the stack
      is verified, then re-cutover. Single bb-portal.ddns.net
      change.
   b. **Cutover atomically** — repoint DNS once, run
      `init-tls.sh` (or `certbot certonly --webroot ...`) on the
      new central, accept the brief outage window between DNS
      flip and certbot completion (~2–5 min).
7. Run `source ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env && ./create-central.sh`.
   Expect fresh-volume bootstrap path; CAS empty, scaler in dry-run by
   default.
8. Smoke-test in dry-run mode (one manual `spawn-worker.sh`, one Bazel
   build with `--remote_cache` only, no executor); flip
   `SCALER_DRY_RUN=false`.
9. Repoint `bb-psmdb.ddns.net` A-record to new FIP (No-IP UI). Wait
   TTL (~5 min). Verify Bazel client → Envoy works end-to-end.
10. Trigger a full Jenkins matrix build on PSMDB-2055 v8.3 to validate.
11. **Rollback ready** for 1–2 weeks: shutdown old central
    (`hcloud server poweroff bb-psmdb-ondemand` in shared project).
    Old volume left attached. DNS revert is a single A-record swap.
12. After 2 weeks of stable operation: delete old VM and volume in
    shared project.

### 9.5 Refactor as separate Git repo (follow-up)

Once §9.4 is stable, the natural next step is to extract
`IaC/buildbarn/` into its own repository (working name
`psmdb-buildbarn` or `percona-buildbarn`):

- One repo = one Hetzner project = one operational unit.
- Lookup-by-name pattern from §10 already supports it (no hardcoded IDs
  to port).
- `jenkins-pipelines` keeps only the Jenkins Groovy / YAML; the BB
  cluster gets its own versioning, its own CI (env validation,
  jsonnet linting), its own release tags.

Out of scope for the migration ticket itself — track separately.

## 10. Operator-local cluster config (lookup-by-name pattern)

Hetzner numeric IDs (`VOLUME_ID`, `NETWORK_ID`, `SSH_KEY_IDS`) are
**project-local** and not safe to commit. Replaced by name-based
lookup + operator-local override file. Names ARE safe to commit
(they're stable and human-readable; the IDs they resolve to live in
the active hcloud auth context).

### 10.1 Repo-side: `create-central.sh` resolution order

```
For each of VOLUME_ID / NETWORK_ID / SSH_KEY_IDS:
  1. If the env var is already set (from cluster.env or a one-shot
     export), use it as-is.
  2. Otherwise: hcloud describe by name (VOLUME_NAME / NETWORK_NAME
     / each entry in SSH_KEY_NAMES) within the currently-authenticated
     Hetzner project. Fail loudly if anything fails to resolve.

Names committed in script:
  VOLUME_NAME    = psmdb-buildbarn-cas-ondemand
  NETWORK_NAME   = psmdb-bazel-rbe
  SSH_KEY_NAMES  = bb-psmdb-laptop bb-psmdb-ondemand-central
```

This makes the script project-portable: same code, same names, the
hcloud token determines which project's IDs are picked up.

### 10.2 Operator-side: `~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env`

Operator-local, never committed. Sourced before each
`create-central.sh` run:

```
source ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env
cd jenkins-pipelines/IaC/buildbarn/ondemand/scripts
./create-central.sh
```

Template:

```bash
# psmdb-bazel-rbe (Hetzner project 14460169) — operator-local config.
# NEVER commit. NEVER share via Slack/email.

# Hetzner auth — pick ONE of these three:
export HCLOUD_TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.config/psmdb-rbe/hcloud.token"   # preferred (file mode 0600)
# export HCLOUD_CONTEXT_NAME=psmdb-bazel-rbe                       # alt: hcloud cli.toml context
# export HCLOUD_TOKEN="..."                                         # least preferred (env leaks)

# Public network identity
export PUBLIC_HOSTNAME=bb-psmdb.ddns.net
export LE_EMAIL=builds-noreply@percona.com

# GitHub OAuth App (Dex GitHub connector). Same App as the shared-project
# deployment — only the callback URL differs (already covered: registered
# under the same hostname).
export GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID="..."
export GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET="..."

# Optional ID overrides — uncomment ONLY if name resolution doesn't suit
# (e.g. duplicate names across projects on the same hcloud token; rare).
# export VOLUME_ID=...
# export NETWORK_ID=...
# export SSH_KEY_IDS="..."
```

Recommended permissions:

```
chmod 700 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe
chmod 600 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/hcloud.token
chmod 600 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env
```

## 11. Known capacity / rate-limit observations

Documented evidence supporting §9 split decision and adjacent backlog
items. Update when new data points arrive.

### 11.1 aarch64 stage start latency in PSMDB-2055 builds

Long aarch64 stage warm-up times (5–20 min before the worker starts
executing actions) traced to two compounding factors:
- **Hetzner API rate-limit pressure** — aarch64 worker spawn requests
  competed with polling traffic from other Jenkins instances in the
  shared project; remaining quota dropped low enough that
  `hcloud server create` calls were briefly stalled.
- **Hetzner aarch64 capacity transient unavailability in `hel1`** —
  several spawn attempts returned "no capacity" until Hetzner freed
  the requested type, independent of our quota.

Both worsen under shared rate-limit; expected to soften noticeably
after §9 split (own quota) but not fully resolved
(aarch64 capacity is upstream of any quota fix).

### 11.2 Central host memory pressure

Snapshots taken under load:
- 15 GiB physical RAM, 93 % utilized at idle.
- 16.1 GiB peak under PSMDB-2055 multi-stage parallel build (swap
  involvement; envoy-proxy observed swapping briefly).
- `storage-0` / `storage-1` (bb-storage shards) consume the largest
  slice — both keep CAS index in mmap'd memory.

Justification basis for a future host bump-up. Track separately —
not blocking the project split.

### 11.3 Envoy circuit-breaker headroom

After §"PSMDB-2034 envoy: lift circuit breakers" patch (max_connections
/ max_pending_requests / max_requests / max_retries → 100 000), all
overflow counters stay at zero under 17 parallel Jenkins stages with
`--jobs=72` each. Verified via `:9901/stats?filter=overflow`.

Notable surprise during observation: `cluster.dex_jwks.upstream_cx_active`
held at ~263 connections — far higher than expected from infrequent
JWKS rotation. Worth a follow-up to confirm Envoy isn't leaking
short-lived JWKS connections, but no functional impact.

